window.harnessChunks["rows-2e72076cbff6d60a.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T22:55:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/iOfficeAI/AionUi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AionUi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/iOfficeAI/AionUi/tree/6744099b279b991c17e31c243f0920477bd31cb6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6744099b279b991c17e31c243f0920477bd31cb6\">6744099 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. AionUi ships a built-in autonomous agent engine and Team Mode runs autonomous Leader/Teammate agents through supported backends. These agents perform the operational file/tool/task work rather than merely exposing constructor slots.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. Team Mode closes a concrete coordination loop across distinct S1s: tasks carry owners and explicit dependency/blocker relations; teammates execute in parallel; and the autonomous Leader decomposes and assigns work while the runtime exposes blocked/queued/running state that changes subsequent team behavior. This regulates duplicate, premature and mutually dependent work rather than merely transporting messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The autonomous Leader has a whole-team current view and authority to assign, track and aggregate teammate work, while Team Mode exposes whole-run/per-slot state and dynamic member control. That establishes first-party agent-owned regulation over current team commitments and progress.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Per-agent approvals, status and ordinary result aggregation are part of the normal execution/control path; no materially independent complementary audit channel with distinct evidence access and corrective closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. External search/tools may support current tasks, but no distinct outside-looking prospective organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human permission dialogs and deployment policy constrain actions, but routine execution approval is not an organizational identity or ultimate-policy tension-and-resolution loop. The historical P therefore does not survive the function-first S5 test.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd/assessments/aionui.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd\">9b431df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Eigent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/tree/6c49956b2aa878c08bee5edcf8a1eb63122c2cd9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6c49956b2aa878c08bee5edcf8a1eb63122c2cd9\">6c49956 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Workforce agents autonomously execute assigned tool/model work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. The task agent produces an explicit dependency graph and runnable work is published only after dependencies are satisfied; the model-driven workforce path selects assignees and reacts to task state. This closes dependency/ordering interference across autonomous S1s rather than merely routing messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The model-driven coordinator has a workforce-wide view and authority over assignment, retries/replanning and current task progression. These are current whole-team regulation decisions owned by an autonomous first-party agent path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. No materially independent complementary audit channel with distinct evidence access and corrective closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Planning and replanning address current task execution; no separate prospective environment-facing organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human permissions/configuration constrain execution but do not establish identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/eigent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:26:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/awslabs/cli-agent-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CLI Agent Orchestrator</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/awslabs/cli-agent-orchestrator/tree/ffa9fc742c7d47ce038d3d951ccc5527ba0c0218\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ffa9fc742c7d47ce038d3d951ccc5527ba0c0218\">ffa9fc7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Multiple model-directed worker sessions implement assigned coding/review tasks and return artifacts/findings to the supervisor through the supported CAO runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, changing code, individual task failures and defects, separate worker provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CAO-started worker agents decide how to perform the delegated task within their advertised developer/reviewer role; the supervisor allocates tasks separately. - Decision owner: Shipped first-party configured agent profiles running model-driven sessions through supported external CLI providers; external provider reasoning details are not credited as first-party code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Profile roles, terminal/provid­er adapter, model process, CAO MCP messages and terminal session manager. - Closure path: Supervisor task/message → first-party-created worker CLI context → worker produces code/review findings → CAO handoff output or inbox callback → supervisor sees completion and selects follow-up. - Boundary reachability: The documented install/launch path exposes built-in codesupervisor; its MCP server can launch developer/reviewer sessions from installed first-party profiles (README.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Work and review choices are generated by actual delegated agent roles, not a deterministic tmux replay; no claim is made about external provider-native internal planning. - Evidence: README.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/developer.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/reviewer.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent damaging shared-source interference among separately acting coding workers when the supported opt-in worktree isolation mode is used. - Distinct S1 units: Independently launched developer/worker terminal sessions via the first-party assign/handoff MCP tools (src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py). - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent spawned workers share a Git checkout by default and can race/overwrite one another&#39;s edits or create merge conflicts; first-party worktree service explicitly identifies these exact hazards (src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py). - Attenuating coordination relation: Supervisor&#39;s opt-in worktree parameter causes CAO to create distinct branch/checkouts for individual child sessions, separating conflicting write contexts; worker results return to the supervisor for explicit integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Worktree-provisioned workers execute in separated checkouts, return task outcome through handoff or inbox callback; supervisor then selects further assignments/repair or integration rather than allowing concurrent edits to the original shared checkout (src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py). - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Evidence ties per-worker isolation directly to an explicitly documented concrete inter-worker file race/overwrite hazard, not to ordinary message delivery or terminal creation alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping mutations and checkout interference between active coding units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The choice to request isolated worktree mode belongs to supervisor/constructor configuration; CAO creates a fresh branch/worktree and enforces the isolated execution context. - Decision owner: First-party optional isolation constructor with developer/supervisor-provided enablement; no autonomous S2-level admission or merge-convergence policy is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-worker server-generated terminal ID, Git worktree creation, per-terminal checkout path, cleanup, MCP assign/handoff callback. - Closure path: Explicit collision risk → assign/handoff with useworktree=True → distinct Git branches and isolated next S1 edits → result delivered to supervisor → subsequent unit work/integration decisions. - Boundary reachability: useworktree is a shipped argument on both first-party MCP tools and forwarded to the local terminal service; createworktree makes an actual branch/checkout (src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Contention-specific relation is built by CAO; the optional activation owner is the supervisor but no independent agent S2 conflict-resolution policy is presumed. - Evidence: src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Positive restricted to explicitly enabled per-worker worktree mode; default shared directory does not receive this S2 closure. No automatic conflict-free merge or code acceptance is claimed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: First-party model-driven codesupervisor governs commitments and rework across its active developer and reviewer worker units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfinished assignments, failed/defective code, availability and outputs of launched agents, shared artifact paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supervisor chooses which worker gets each task, when findings trigger rework and when review is satisfactory. - Decision owner: Bundled codesupervisor model role, not the tmux scheduler, status endpoint or underlying provider&#39;s hidden native agent hierarchy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CAO assign/handoff, result callbacks, inbox delivery/status, supervisor file/task directory rules. - Whole-system current view: Within the expressly bounded supervisor team, supervisor tracks assigned tasks, known code artifacts, callbacks and reviewer findings, not all sessions/fleets of external agents. - Current-control decision scope: Developer/reviewer assignment, repair/retry commitments and task convergence at the supervisor-team recursion. - Closure path: Subordinate outcomes and reviewer feedback → supervisor checks team current work and chooses assignment/retry/approval → new worker work and returned completion. - Boundary reachability: cao install codesupervisor and cao launch --agents codesupervisor are documented; its first-party role/instructions require task assignment, progress tracking, rework on failed review, and delegate through wired MCP tools (README.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervisor decides commitments based on returned worker/reviewer observations; tool transport/terminal service only enforces dispatch and delivery. - Evidence: src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py. - Basis: explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A distinct code-review worker audits produced source/code artifacts and sends independent problem findings back into the supervisor&#39;s repair/acceptance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code quality, correctness, missing test coverage, security vulnerabilities in developer-produced work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dedicated reviewer model makes its own source-inspection judgment; supervisor decides whether findings require developer repair. - Decision owner: Separate built-in reviewer model session for audit judgment; supervisor owns follow-up commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party reviewer role/tool-access limits, shared artifact paths, CAO handoff/assign and return message mechanism. - Claim being audited: Developer completion/quality claims about newly produced project files. - Ordinary reporting path: Developer sends work artifacts/status to the CAO supervisor. - Complementary access path: A separately instantiated read-oriented reviewer receives source paths and inspects code independently of the developer&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: Separate worker terminal/profile, read-oriented reviewer tools and independently model-produced findings; it is neither the developer&#39;s own loop nor a deterministic assertion of completion. - Who acts on findings: Supervisor relays defects to developer, repeats review and decides when satisfied. - Closure path: Developer artifact → separate reviewer source examination → findings to supervisor → subsequent developer correction/re-review before finishing. - Boundary reachability: Built-in supervisor explicitly requires a code-review worker after development and repair loops; the shipped reviewer profile has a review-only role and sends findings back over the first-party MCP channel (src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/reviewer.md; docs/tool-restrictions.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct model reviewer produces content-specific defect judgments; model independence is role/process-level, not proof of different provider weights or guaranteed statistical independence. - Evidence: src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/reviewer.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Positive only in the bundled developer+reviewer supervisor mode, not every launch. Reviewer is independently running but no claim is made of organizational independence outside this task-level CAO team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Optional learning/outcome/retrospector/instruction-promotion path might adapt future agents, but its external-prospective S4 function and decisive owner remain unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Recurring historical workflow failures and future task differences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Retrospector stores lessons; separate operator must explicitly apply promoted lessons into agent profiles, but it is unproven whether this constitutes full outside-and-then intelligence rather than retrospective process optimization. - Decision owner: Mixed autonomous retrospector and operator for promotion; no complete S4 autonomous or first-party parent mode established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Outcome records, memoryrecall, retrospector profile, opt-in learning flags, cao memory promote --apply. - External distinction: Work outcomes can carry evidence of changing task environments, but the explicit external opportunity/threat prospective appraisal is not established. - Future / prospective distinction: Promoted lessons affect future new sessions, unlike an ordinary retry. - Adaptation option generated: Retrospector may distill up to three reusable lessons; human-triggered promotion writes them to future profiles. - Path back into current capability / S3: Profile learned patterns apply on later sessions; first-party prospective external intelligence judgment/authority boundary still under review. - Closure path: Reported outcome → retrospector lesson → user promotion → future profile instructions, without an established independent outside-and-then appraisal. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic memory capture and config editing do not by themselves establish the organizational S4 function. - Evidence: docs/self-learning.md; docs/self-learning.md; docs/self-learning.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/promotionservice.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: First-party tool roles and operator approval policy restrict action, but ultimate project identity/organizational policy authority return path remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool permissions, agent identities and allowed delegation, workflow safety/custody and operator oversight. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/deployer selects launch permissions and approvals; roles bound available MCP/CLI actions. - Decision owner: Operator for safety and profile policy; model supervisor chooses within constraints. No S5-specific parent closure established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-based allowedTools, profile schema and permission checks, workflow plan approval gate with opt-in setting and persistent approved plan ID. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Accepted tool/role policy of the CAO session and approved workflow intent. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Not credited; operator can approve specific actions, but project-wide identity/policy decision loop has not been established. - Return-to-operation path: Profile/tool restrictions affect what worker may call; workflow approval gates permit/deny runs. These are enforcement/approval paths, not proven ultimate-policy closure. - Closure path: Agent attempts tools → permission/approval checks → allowed/blocked execution; unresolved higher-level policy decision ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only reviewer role and guarded workflow approval do not turn static constraints or generic human approval into S5. - Evidence: docs/tool-restrictions.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/approvalgate.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1dc2d2e352399be6e8e0ae673bd65d038049a708/assessments/cli-agent-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1dc2d2e352399be6e8e0ae673bd65d038049a708\">1dc2d2e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Deep Agents</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/tree/f86b4e9abef7620b63a7258bc9fab0ccd83de8a4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f86b4e9abef7620b63a7258bc9fab0ccd83de8a4\">f86b4e9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary and delegated agents autonomously perform model/tool work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: concurrent async tasks, isolation and lifecycle APIs establish multiple S1 units but no specific first-party inter-S1 interference/oscillation plus attenuation relation. Start/update/cancel/list are hierarchical current-control rights, not mutual-adjustment evidence by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the main agent has a live view of its tracked background commitments and model-facing authority to start, inspect, redirect/interupt, cancel and list those commitments. The decisive intervention is chosen by the autonomous main agent; SDK/LangGraph machinery transports and enforces it. This closes a current whole-subagent-set regulation loop without a developer having to supply another control actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: a separate rubric grader challenges a candidate completion and can independently gather read-only evidence through the first-party dcode wiring. needsrevision findings are returned into the primary loop and cause further work. This is materially complementary access plus autonomous audit judgment and feedback closure, rather than ordinary inline QA.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: rubric iteration, memory, skills and task updates concern present execution/quality. No separate prospective environment-facing loop that changes future organizational capability/strategy was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: HITL/interrupton, rubrics and runtime options can gate operational actions or define acceptance criteria, but they do not establish identity/ultimate-policy closure. Generic human interruption is therefore not S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/deepagents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Qwen Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code/tree/3ac6fe65e81c84897c02d22abd3dc91d1e567d5f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3ac6fe65e81c84897c02d22abd3dc91d1e567d5f\">3ac6fe6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: coding agents autonomously perform tool-using work inside their assigned scope.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: multiple S1 teammates share task ownership and messages. Atomic claiming prevents duplicate task ownership, priority delivery changes subsequent teammate behavior, and bounded pending-message queues attenuate a concrete flood/interference mode. Agents choose task/message actions while TeamManager provides deterministic enforcement and feedback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the team leader is an autonomous current-control actor over the team. It can create/direct teammates and invoke a leader-only shutdown that removes a teammate from participation and releases its task commitment; TeamManager supplies the whole-team state/lifecycle substrate and closes the intervention. Separately, Qwen Code&#39;s interactive approval mode can require the parent user to allow/deny these ask-classified team-control actions before execution, establishing a distinct parent-governed S3 mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no distinct complementary audit actor/path with materially different access to operational reality and findings returned into current control was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: no separate outside-and-future adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: approval modes and team-action permissions govern operational interventions. They do not establish a first-party identity/ultimate-policy question and legitimate ultimate-authority loop, so the previous generic S5=P interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/qwen-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-framework\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Agent Framework</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-16\">2026-09-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-framework/tree/1cd06c5a2058a172eebadf5d9d7c3fa45c519520\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1cd06c5a2058a172eebadf5d9d7c3fa45c519520\">1cd06c5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: specialist agents autonomously perform bounded tool/model work and return operational outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the Magentic manager actively detects loop/stall/no-progress conditions and changes team execution to restore convergence. This is agent-owned regulation of instability among operational units, not static graph topology.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the default Magentic manager owns a whole-team current-state ledger of facts/plan/progress, chooses who acts next, and has reset/replan authority over current commitments, establishing an autonomous S3 mode. The first-party plan-review option establishes a second parent-governed mode over the same S3 function: before a plan executes, a human sees the current progress ledger and proposed whole-team plan and may approve it or require revision; revision is returned to the manager, which replans before work continues. This is materially different from participant tool-call approval because it controls the team&#39;s current plan itself.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the same manager that regulates current work observes progress; no organizationally distinct complementary audit channel with independent access/authority was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: replanning responds to current execution failure. The reviewed implementation does not establish a separate prospective external-environment intelligence function that adapts future system capability/strategy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: goals, participants, instructions and orchestration policy remain application-defined. Human signoff on a current Magentic plan is S3 current-control authority, not by itself ultimate organizational identity or policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/microsoft-agent-framework.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matiasrodlo/veritas\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Veritas</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matiasrodlo/veritas/tree/f366432381834dee8ecb17bcbc2bad3e6232ff0b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f366432381834dee8ecb17bcbc2bad3e6232ff0b\">f366432 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: generate, debug, execute, compare, and refine experiment implementations to gather scientific evidence for the loaded research idea. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code/runtime failures, inadequate implementations, hyperparameter uncertainty, poor metrics, scaling choices, and uncertainty about which experimental change best tests the hypothesis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose candidate implementation changes and subsequent experiment actions in response to execution errors, journal history, metrics, and stage goals. - Decision owner: the model-driven tree-search research agent (ParallelAgent/candidate-generation path). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated interpreter, execution timeout/resource controls, journals/tree nodes, named metrics and provenance, multi-seed evaluation, artifacts/plots, search configuration, and checkpoints. - Closure path: generated code is executed; errors/metrics/artifacts return into the search journal and prompts; subsequent nodes implement revisions or alternative experiments until stage criteria/limits are reached. - Boundary reachability: the tree-search agent and interpreter are the core path invoked by launchscientistbfts.py; no external agent harness supplies the experiment loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic execution and metric parsing constrain/measure candidates, but the model-driven agent chooses substantive implementation and experiment changes. - Evidence: README search description, tree-search runner, AgentManager agent creation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parallel candidates/tree branches are alternative attempts inside the operational research process; their plurality is not itself evidence of S2.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific inter-S1 coordination loop is established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker isolation, process/resource limits, stage sequencing, shared journals, and candidate ranking prevent contamination or structure search but do not evidence a concrete interference/oscillation among distinct cooperating S1 units. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrent or branching experiment attempts are not automatically separate operational units requiring S2; they are candidate searches for one S1 research outcome. - Evidence: README execution/isolation description, experiment runner. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a future multi-researcher mode would require a distinct S1 mapping and explicit interference/attenuation witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current evidence about the whole active research search/stage and decide completion, next-substage focus, and progression of the experiment program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stagnating search, unmet stage success criteria, unresolved implementation issues, insufficient improvement, under-scaled experiments, and uncertainty about the most useful next search focus. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the current stage/substage is complete and generate specific goals/focus for the next substage from current progress/issues/metrics. - Decision owner: AgentManager&#39;s model-driven feedback/evaluation path for discretionary completion/focus judgments, operating within deterministic minimum/max/adaptive-stopping constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full stage journal, best-node selection, success-criteria checks, improvement counters, deterministic stopping rules, stage history, checkpoints, and stage lifecycle code. - Closure path: current journal metrics/issues/recent changes are summarized into the feedback model; the resulting completion decision or generated substage goals alter stage lifecycle and the prompt/goals used by the next research agent. - Boundary reachability: AgentManager is the standard controller instantiated by the main tree-search runner, and its feedback model/path is configured as part of normal experiment execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed timeouts/max-iterations and metric checks are enforcement. S3 credit comes from the additional model-driven current-control judgment that interprets current whole-stage evidence and creates the next research focus. - Whole-system current view: active stage journal, all attempts in that stage, good/buggy nodes, best metric/node, success-criteria progress, current issues/recent changes, stage history, and prior-stage best implementation when relevant. - Current-control decision scope: stage completion, continuation versus progression, and specific next-substage research goals/focus that determine subsequent resource/effort commitment within the run. - Evidence: AgentManager completion logic, next-substage goal generation, standard runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every AgentManager decision is autonomous: many limits/checks are deterministic. The positive state depends specifically on the model-driven completion and next-focus decisions wired into current operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material operationally closed complementary-audit loop is established in the reviewed full-run path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent audit right with corrective return is established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: metric provenance, multi-seed checks, plot/VLM checks, zero-data abort, and final automated paper review provide validation/review surfaces. - Closure path: the final review is written after the experiment search and paper generation, but the reviewed launcher does not feed its findings back into AgentManager, write-up, or experiments for corrective operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: performreview is a separate review action by name, but S3 requires complementary audit plus findings-to-control closure. Post-hoc persisted review without return is insufficient. - Evidence: launcher review path, README output including reviewtext.txt. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: internal VLM/metric validation is part of ordinary search acceptance/current control, while the separate final review lacks corrective return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: in the supported idea-generation mode, sense external scientific literature, reflect on novelty/feasibility, and produce a future experiment program that the standard research run can execute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk that a proposed research direction duplicates existing work, misses relevant prior findings, or lacks a feasible/meaningful experimental plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/refine/finalize the research proposal after incorporating Semantic Scholar retrieval into repeated idea reflection. - Decision owner: the model-driven idea-generation agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party Semantic Scholar tool, multi-round reflection history, prior-idea archive, structured FinalizeIdea schema, and instruction requiring at least one literature search before finalization. - Closure path: external literature results enter the reflection loop; the finalized idea includes related work, hypothesis, experiments, and risk factors; the resulting JSON is directly consumable by launchscientistbfts.py, where it determines subsequent staged experimental capability/work. - Boundary reachability: README documents performideationtempfree.py as the first-party way to create an ideas file from a short workshop brief; its output is the same schema consumed by the standard launcher. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the literature tool retrieves external distinctions, but the model-driven ideation loop owns proposal selection/refinement; the experiment launcher then operationalizes the returned option. - External distinction: retrieved Semantic Scholar prior work and related-work information outside the current Veritas run. - Future / prospective distinction: novelty, feasibility, and which hypothesis/experimental direction should be pursued in later stages. - Adaptation option generated: finalized research idea containing hypothesis, related work, experiment plan, and risks/limitations. - Path back into current capability / S3: the idea JSON becomes the launcher&#39;s task description; AgentManager derives stage work from its experiment plan and executes the resulting research program. - Evidence: idea-generation tool and required literature search, README documented idea generation, launcher idea loading. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the internal creativeresearch stage and reuse of previous experiment results are not the basis for S4. Credit depends on the distinct first-party external-literature → future idea → launched experiment closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established at the declared research-run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user/workshop idea, structured idea schema, stage goals, configuration, model choices, resource limits, and review/write-up settings constrain operation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is shown reaching a legitimate authority and returning as a binding decision to subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: idea generation chooses the scientific direction to pursue, which is credited as S4; it does not evidence authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity/governance policy at runtime. - Evidence: idea schema and launcher, AgentManager stage contract. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human selection of the input idea/configuration is not automatically parent-governed S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/veritas.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The AI Scientist-v2</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v2/tree/96bd51617cfdbb494a9fc283af00fe090edfae48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 96bd51617cfdbb494a9fc283af00fe090edfae48\">96bd516 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform executable ML research experiments through model-driven proposal/code changes, execution and feedback-guided search. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation failures, changing metrics/plots, alternative model/data choices, debugging needs and experimental outcomes across tree-search nodes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete experiment/code changes and search-node actions based on current task description, parent node/history and execution feedback. - Decision owner: first-party parallel experiment/search agents instantiated by AgentManager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: journals/nodes, experiment executor callbacks, workspace isolation/state, stage-specific task descriptions, search budgets and GPU/process management. - Closure path: research-stage goal/current node -&gt; agent proposes implementation/experiment -&gt; runtime executes it -&gt; metrics/errors/plots are recorded in journal state -&gt; agent/search policy chooses subsequent node/action. - Boundary reachability: the tree-search agents are created and run by the shipped AgentManager used directly by launchscientistbfts.py. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: schedulers/GPU management enforce execution resources, but the context-sensitive experimental action is selected by model-driven agents. - Evidence: README.md, agentmanager.py, parallelagent.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material agent-owned S2 coordination path is established among the parallel S1 search workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers share finite execution resources and contribute nodes to experiment search, but no agent-owned interference/oscillation detector and attenuation relation is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel worker scheduling, GPU manager/allocation, journals and tree-search selection. - Closure path: resource/scheduling code assigns/releases execution capacity and search code selects nodes; this is deterministic orchestration rather than an S2 conflict-regulation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker plurality and GPU allocation are not promoted to S2 without an agent-owned response to a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: parallelagent.py, agentmanager.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-experiment current view and adaptively control stage completion, continuation and next experimental focus on behalf of the research run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or unstable implementations, insufficient dataset coverage, weak progress, missing stage criteria, changing best-node evidence and current experimental issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether the current sub-stage/stage is complete and generate measurable next sub-stage goals/focus from current experiment evidence; advance or continue the staged research program accordingly. - Decision owner: the model-driven feedback/control queries executed by the first-party AgentManager over its whole current stage/journal state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage/journal state, best-node selection, VLM plot feedback, structured completion function schemas, hard bounds/fallback rules, checkpointing and stage-transition machinery. - Closure path: current journals/best node/metrics/plot feedback -&gt; manager control query judges completion or identifies missing criteria -&gt; manager continues current stage or creates a new focused sub-stage/main stage -&gt; subsequent agents receive the revised stage goals and operate under them. - Boundary reachability: AgentManager is instantiated by the standard BFTS experiment path and its run() loop directly creates worker agents and applies the completion/focus decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed stage ordering and max-iteration bounds are deterministic support, but the materially adaptive completion/focus judgments are model queries that change subsequent whole-run experiment commitments. - Whole-system current view: current stage, all stage journals/history, best implementation, success counts/metrics, VLM feedback, tested datasets and current issues/progress at the declared experiment-run recursion. - Current-control decision scope: whether to continue/complete the current stage and what concrete goals/focus the next sub-stage should impose on all subsequent experiment workers. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, performexperimentsbftswithagentmanager.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary audit loop returning sufficiently independent findings into the S3 current-control path is established at the reviewed revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: VLM plot analysis, multi-seed evaluation and final paper review provide evaluation signals, but their roles do not establish a separate operational audit channel with corrective return to S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plot/VLM feedback, multi-seed evaluation, LLM text review and image/caption/reference review. - Closure path: VLM/metric feedback is consumed as part of ordinary manager evaluation; final paper reviews are written to files after write-up and are not returned into a correction/re-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluator agents/models exist, but evaluation naming or separate inference alone is insufficient without a complementary independence-and-corrective-return closure. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, launchscientistbfts.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense external literature and prospective scientific opportunity, generate/refine a future research proposal, and return that option into current experiment execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prior literature may make ideas non-novel; alternative hypotheses/experiments may differ in feasibility, impact and relation to existing work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose search queries, incorporate retrieved literature into reflection, and finalize a structured future proposal/hypothesis/experiment plan. - Decision owner: first-party ideation LLM agent using the shipped Semantic Scholar tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SemanticScholarSearchTool, action/tool protocol, reflection loop, structured FinalizeIdea schema and JSON handoff to launcher. - Closure path: workshop/topic description -&gt; ideation agent searches external literature -&gt; retrieved results alter reflection/proposal -&gt; agent finalizes structured idea -&gt; launchscientistbfts.py consumes that idea and executes the experiment program. - Boundary reachability: performideationtempfree.py is the documented first step for generating the JSON consumed by the standard experiment launcher. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the search tool supplies evidence, but the prospective research-option synthesis and finalization are agent decisions. - External distinction: Semantic Scholar literature and related-work evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: novelty, feasibility, risks and which hypothesis/experiments should be pursued next. - Adaptation option generated: structured research proposal containing hypothesis, related work, experiments and limitations. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated idea JSON becomes the task description for AgentManager and its experiment stages. - Evidence: performideationtempfree.py, README.md, launchscientistbfts.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workshop/topic descriptions, stage goals, risk-factor prompts, model choices and BFTS configuration constrain research, but they do not form an identity-level ultimate-authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workshop description, proposal schema, hard-coded main-stage goals, YAML search configuration and CLI arguments. - Closure path: configuration/prompt policy directly constrains the run; no identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated to an authoritative actor and returned as governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent manager can adapt current experimental focus, but it is not given authority to redefine the system&#39;s identity or ultimate research policy. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, performideationtempfree.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/ai-scientist-v2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The AI Scientist</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist/tree/1de1dbc1f4ee2c5f61e9c94348d55eb51d7fa2eb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1de1dbc1f4ee2c5f61e9c94348d55eb51d7fa2eb\">1de1dbc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement and execute research experiments and produce the resulting scientific artifacts within a maintained experiment template. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code/runtime errors, differing experimental ideas, metric outcomes, plotting/write-up needs and project-file state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete experiment/code edits and subsequent actions based on the idea, baseline state and observed execution/results. - Decision owner: the active model-driven Aider coding/experiment agent used by the first-party experiment loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: copied per-idea workspace, baseline results, performexperiments, Aider file editing, process execution, notes/logs and bounded experiment flow. - Closure path: idea/baseline/workspace -&gt; agent edits experiment implementation -&gt; code executes -&gt; result/error evidence returns -&gt; agent chooses further experiment edits or completion. - Boundary reachability: the loop is invoked by the documented launchscientist.py entrypoint for every novel idea in a standard paper-generation run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Python launcher logic enforces sequencing, but the context-sensitive experiment/code choices are model-agent decisions. - Evidence: launchscientist.py, README.md, aiscientist/performexperiments.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple ideas may execute in parallel on different GPUs, but the workers are independent attempts; no concrete cross-S1 conflict/oscillation and attenuation relation is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multiprocessing queue, GPU assignment and independent per-idea workspaces. - Closure path: scheduling distributes independent jobs; it does not return a coordination decision to change behavior because of inter-S1 interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resource scheduling is deterministic runtime support and parallelism alone is not S2. - Evidence: launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failures/success at experiment/write-up/review stages change control flow, but stage ordering and stop/advance rules are fixed by the launcher. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-run current view and discretionary authority over current shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: launcher stage sequence, exceptions/return values, GPU/process setup, skip/improvement flags and fixed success gates. - Closure path: deterministic success/failure and CLI configuration drive stage transitions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local agents decide experiments/write-up content, while whole-run control remains scripted. - Evidence: launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the produced research manuscript and, in the supported improvement mode, return findings into a separate corrective write-up path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported/unclear claims, presentation weaknesses, methodological weaknesses and other defects observable by a paper reviewer but not necessarily corrected during ordinary write-up. - Decisive decision or feedback right: produce a structured peer-review judgment/weakness set over the completed paper; those findings become explicit corrective input in improvement mode. - Decision owner: the first-party automated paper-review invocation, separate from the Aider writer/coder session being reviewed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PDF loading, review ensemble/reflections, persisted review.txt, performimprovement, PDF regeneration and second review. - Closure path: writer produces PDF -&gt; separate reviewer evaluates it -&gt; structured review/weaknesses -&gt; performimprovement(review, coder) returns findings to writer/coder -&gt; revised PDF -&gt; reviewer evaluates the revised artifact again. - Boundary reachability: --improvement is an explicit supported launcher mode using first-party review and improvement functions; it requires no external custom orchestration beyond normal model credentials. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer owns the semantic challenge judgment; the launcher only transfers review findings and enforces the second pass. - Claim being audited: that the research manuscript adequately presents/supports the generated scientific work at peer-review quality. - Ordinary reporting path: experiment notes/results -&gt; manuscript generation by the coding/writing agent. - Complementary access path: a separate review invocation reads the rendered paper and evaluates it with a review-specific prompt/ensemble rather than reusing the writer conversation. - Independence boundary: writer/coder and reviewer are separate model interactions/roles; review is generated after the artifact is materialized. - Who acts on findings: the Aider writer/coder through performimprovement, followed by manuscript regeneration. - Evidence: launchscientist.py, aiscientist/performreview.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: generate prospective research options, interrogate external literature for novelty and return novel ideas into current experimentation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing prior work may make a candidate idea non-novel; alternative ideas differ in feasibility/interestingness and should change the next experiment attempted. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose/refine research ideas and, during novelty checking, choose literature queries and decide whether each idea is novel enough to enter the experiment set. - Decision owner: first-party idea-generation/novelty-checking model agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idea reflection archive, Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex search, structured idea JSON, novelty flag and launcher filtering to novelideas. - Closure path: template/task context -&gt; agent proposes/refines idea -&gt; external literature searches return evidence -&gt; novelty judgment marks idea -&gt; only novel ideas are dispatched into executable experiments. - Boundary reachability: idea generation and novelty checking are invoked by the standard launcher unless explicitly skipped; their JSON/novelty outputs directly gate experiment admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: search APIs provide external observations, but query selection, idea refinement and novelty judgment are model-agent decisions. - External distinction: retrieved titles/abstracts/metadata from Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a proposed idea is sufficiently novel/feasible/interesting to justify a future experiment. - Adaptation option generated: structured experiment idea with title, experiment outline and quality ratings. - Path back into current capability / S3: launcher filters ideas to novelideas and runs each accepted idea through doidea experiment/write-up execution. - Evidence: aiscientist/generateideas.py, launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: experiment templates, prompt/task descriptions, model/engine flags and safety/container advice constrain operation, but no identity-level issue is routed to an ultimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: template prompt.json, CLI options, maintained template structure and static safety guidance. - Closure path: configuration/prompt/template policy directly constrains work; no identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research agents can select ideas/experiments but do not own authority to redefine the system&#39;s organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md, launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/ai-scientist.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:48:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LLaMAR</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR/tree/d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\">d2b74ca &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute the embodied work required to complete the shared task through robot-specific actions in the environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial observations, object locations/states, navigation/manipulation requirements, action failures and changing environment state during a long-horizon episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose each robot&#39;s next substantive environment action from the current observations/state and interpret returned success/failure when selecting what happens next. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven LLaMAR action/controller path over the embodied robot streams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AI2-THOR/SAR environment adapters, feasible-action mapping, per-agent observations/states/action histories, task/subtask state and deterministic execution plumbing. - Closure path: team task/current plan -&gt; model selects an action for each robot -&gt; environment executes each embodied action -&gt; per-agent observation/state/success/failure returns -&gt; later model call selects changed robot actions -&gt; episode progresses. - Boundary reachability: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py and SAR/baselines/llamar.py are shipped runnable first-party episode entrypoints and directly execute the credited action/environment feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: simulator code transports actions and state, while the substantive next-action choices are model-generated from the live episode context. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: next-action authority is centralized in the model call rather than separately modelled inside each physical robot, but the operational behavior is still first-party agent-owned at the assessed team boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among simultaneously operating embodied robots sharing the same physical workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robots blocking/bumping one another, congestion around shared objects/receptacles, one robot&#39;s manipulation/navigation preventing another robot from completing its assigned action, and unnecessary simultaneous movement into the same constrained area. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose joint next-step actions such as waiting, moving aside, closing an obstruction or delaying one robot so another robot can proceed without cross-agent interference. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven joint action/controller path using whole-team state and failure context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared observations/state, previous action/failure fields, combined task/memory state, explicit anti-blocking prompt constraints and per-robot joint action output. - Closure path: multiple robot operations interact in one room -&gt; execution exposes cross-agent blocking/failure -&gt; the next model call reasons about the interference -&gt; assigns compensating actions across the involved robots -&gt; later robot behavior changes and the blocked operation can proceed. - Boundary reachability: the standard LLaMAR actor prompt is called on every episode step from the runnable baseline and directly receives/returns the team state and per-agent joint actions used by env.step(). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-interference choice is contextual model reasoning over current cross-agent state; deterministic code only executes the selected joint actions. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic task allocation is not the basis for S2; the positive state rests on the separately evidenced physical interference problem and feedback relation. - Distinct S1 units: the concurrently acting embodied robot streams (Alice, Bob and additional configured robots), each with its own observation/state/action/success history and physical action effect. - Inter-S1 disturbance: one robot can physically block/bump another or occupy/open an object/path such that another robot&#39;s navigation/manipulation fails or must wait. - Attenuating coordination relation: the joint controller sees all robots&#39; state/history and is explicitly instructed to avoid blocking/bumping; failure reasoning can prescribe wait/move-aside/clear-path behavior across the involved robots. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the model&#39;s coordinated per-robot action dictionary is passed through actionchecker and then to env.step(), changing the next embodied action of each robot. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the decisive witness is regulation of a concrete interaction disturbance among simultaneous embodied operations, not the existence of multiple robots, shared memory or a task decomposition graph.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain the whole team&#39;s current task commitments and intervene in the active multi-robot plan as completion/failure/environment information changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing team progress, completed versus still-open commitments, newly observed environment information and failures requiring the active plan or current robot allocation/actions to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the current shared open-subtask plan for the team and, through the whole-team action/controller stage, determine which operational action each robot should take now under that plan. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven planner and whole-team action/controller path. - Whole-system current view: the planner receives the task, current observation from every configured robot, and the shared open/completed subtask state after each verifier step; the action/controller additionally receives every robot&#39;s state, previous action/failure, shared subtasks and combined memory. - Current-control decision scope: preserve or revise the team&#39;s active unfinished commitments and assign the next concrete actions/subtasks across the current robot population, including interventions after failures and current spatial conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared open/completed-subtask state, verifier updates, whole-team observation aggregation, combined memory, planner prompt, action/controller prompt and repeated plan-update loop. - Closure path: robot operations execute -&gt; team observations/actions and completion state update -&gt; verifier updates completed commitments -&gt; planner forms a revised whole-team open plan -&gt; action/controller maps that current plan/state to robot-specific next actions -&gt; changed operations execute and report back. - Boundary reachability: the planner/action stages are invoked in every standard runnable LLaMAR episode; no external orchestrator is required to form the whole-team view or return interventions into robot operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic choice of the current plan and per-robot intervention is model-driven; deterministic code stores plan/completion state and transports decisions into the environment. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 credit is not based on the component name Planner; it rests on the whole-team current view plus returned decisions changing the active shared commitments and robot actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: the verifier provides in-band completion reporting, but no materially independent complementary-audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect assumptions about whether a subtask has completed can be corrected before replanning, but the checked claim remains part of the ordinary task-progress loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor with a distinct authority/evidence channel is established; the verifier prompt only classifies completed subtasks for the same episode. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3; the verifier uses the ordinary model service/configuration and shared episode evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt, all robot observations/states/previous actions, shared open/completed subtasks and combined memory. - Closure path: robot actions execute -&gt; ordinary episode observations feed verifier -&gt; completed-subtask list feeds planner -&gt; current plan changes; this is ordinary progress reporting/monitoring without an evidenced independent audit boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a semantic verifier exists, but verifier naming and a separate prompt-stage do not establish complementary independence. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the verifier does influence subsequent planning; the missing requirement is organizational/complementary independence, not lack of feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective capability-adaptation loop is established beyond current-episode planning/replanning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: LLaMAR adapts its current task plan to new observations and failures, but does not evidence an actor that senses an external future environment, generates capability-change options and installs a change for later operational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective adaptation of the installed harness/robot organization across episodes. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current observation updates, planner replanning, memory and failure reasoning. - Closure path: environment change/failure -&gt; current episode replanning -&gt; changed immediate robot actions; no future-oriented capability option is implemented into later installed capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current replanning is S1/S3 regulation, not evidence of S4 merely because the plan changes. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: long-horizon planning and replanning are substantial capabilities but remain inside the present episode boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity or ultimate-policy authority is established at the multi-robot episode boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task text, model/configuration parameters, prompt rules and simulator limits constrain behavior but do not constitute identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining or reconciling the team&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose/policy under competing S3/S4 demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-selected task/scene/configuration, static prompts and runtime constraints. - Closure path: external task/configuration enters directly as operating constraints; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the models control current operational/coordination/management choices but do not choose the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or policy. - Evidence: README.md; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static system prompts describing robot roles/constraints are not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563/assessments/llamar.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563\">a6735e4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:28:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgenticX</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX/tree/582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\">582c9d0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended user tasks by choosing tools, using execution results, and modifying task artifacts through root/child provider-backed agent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task details, tool errors, repository content, provider responses and subtask outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next model tool call, interpret feedback and continue/revise an execution plan. - Decision owner: configured model-backed AgentRuntime/ReAct root and spawned agents; neither the SDK Agent data model nor the event queue owns model reasoning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider resolver, agent/tool loop, Studio session persistence, subagent context/tool permissions and callbacks. - Closure path: model chooses a tool, runtime executes it, the result enters the same agent&#39;s context, and later decisions change task output. - Boundary reachability: this path is shipped both through the agx/Studio local backend and embedded SDK; Near&#39;s default app starts this local runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent model makes contextual tool/continuation decisions; host code supplies guardrails and transport. - Evidence: AgentRuntime, ReActAgent, Studio server Meta-Agent setup. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: successful live provider/model execution and claimed production quality were not measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidence-backed distinct relation that autonomously attenuates an identified interference among simultaneously acting S1 units, beyond task partition and bounded queueing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential overlap of parallel workers, resource contention and conflicting changes would require interference-specific handling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent-owned cross-worker conflict detection, reconciliation or isolation decision shown as an operationally closed coordination loop for the declared Studio team. - Decision owner: none established at S2; the model planner assigns tasks and host TaskLock transports actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team task decomposition, worker assignments/dependencies, TaskLock action queue, group routing and shared context. - Closure path: delegations can complete and summaries return, but that is ordinary task routing; no distinct detected inter-S1 collision and corrective relation is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming a CoordinatorAgent or serializing actions does not establish function-specific S2 conflict attenuation. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model-backed child agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-file edits or contested resources are plausible, but the inspected supported team path does not independently establish a response to an observed conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: no qualifying inter-S1 relation established; task lists and a queue prevent generic scheduling races, not semantically competing work ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: worker results and messages return to the manager/leader, but without a verified conflict-specific control decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2-specific ownership is not credited for generic dispatch or action queue operations. - Evidence: Team mode wiring and TaskLock, TaskLock, WorkforcePattern. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: developers may compose custom S2 adapters with the SDK; that is not automatically the shipped Studio default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root-session team by observing children and deciding when to spawn, communicate, retry or cancel during live task delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active child runs may stall/fail, saturate concurrency or CPU/memory, need new work or require intervention to meet the immediate user task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and invoke status query, spawn/cancel/retry/message subagent tools in light of reported current child state and goals. - Decision owner: configured root model-backed Meta-Agent within the supported Studio session, not the AgentTeamManager or the deterministic ResourceMonitor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTeamManager actual live status registry, timeouts, caps and resource admission checks; root Meta-Agent tool dispatcher; Studio event stream. - Closure path: root sees team status/tool results, chooses a current-team intervention, AgentTeamManager changes a child run/permission to launch, and updated state returns to the root&#39;s next model turn. - Boundary reachability: Studio builds the root Meta-Agent with visible team-management tools, and the attached AgentTeamManager executes them on real session children in the shipped local backend. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current worker-team decisions are controlled by the root model, while deterministic caps merely reject inadmissible actions. - Whole-system current view: querysubagentstatus and team status provide running/completed/failed child state and resource-monitor context for the session team. - Current-control decision scope: the current portfolio of active/retryable child agents in one root session, including admission and cessation; not Enterprise gateway quotas or an unrelated Studio user. - Evidence: team lifecycle/status/cancel, meta tool handler, resource monitor. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the model might choose not to use these tools; the claim is an available integrated operational loop, not a guaranteed policy of continuous proactive optimization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently controlled complementary reality-audit path with corrective control established for the current Studio session team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an executor can mistakenly report complete work while the real environment differs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor&#39;s direct-evidence veto/intervention against the team report established in the selected operating mode. - Decision owner: none established; SDK evaluation/LLM judge and Enterprise audit logging are separate or routine surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: traces, tool results, observability and optional evaluation framework. - Closure path: tests/evaluations can be explicitly launched, but a first-party independently owned standing complementary team audit with returned corrective control is not demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluation APIs, group worker results and log chains cannot substitute for a separate auditor owner. - Evidence: evaluation/observability overview, team results, Enterprise gateway separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: externally composed independent reviewers are possible through the SDK, but their independence cannot be credited to the shipped session team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: convert observations of changing external tool/provider/task conditions and user corrections into future reusable skill capability for subsequent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated external tool failures, newly learned environment conventions and multi-step strategies that existing installed skills do not yet encode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the background model-backed review agent decides whether and how to create/patch a reusable skill; under the normal supervised mode the Desktop operator separately decides whether to approve the proposed future capability. - Decision owner: opt-in skill-review agent in the direct-authorized mode (A), and legitimate Desktop-user approver for a separately supported queued-proposal mode (P). These modes are not simultaneous autonomous governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered SessionReviewHook, actual tool observation records, signal/complexity gate, configured review model, skillmanage, skill policy scan, pending proposals and desktop approval endpoint. - Closure path: a completed tool-using session triggers a review of observed attempts/errors/user corrections; the model proposes a skill; in explicitly authorized direct mode it writes the skill for later sessions, while in default queue mode the Desktop user approves the same proposed document and installs it. Later model runs can discover/use the changed skill collection. - External distinction: observations concern user corrections and tool/provider outcomes from the task environment, not just the host runtime&#39;s own counters; the model interprets their reusable implications. - Future / prospective distinction: a post-session reviewer encodes a method for later task contexts rather than repairing only the completed current task, and the installed skill survives that session boundary. - Adaptation option generated: choose a new action-oriented skill or a patch to an existing skill, including the specific steps and guardrails for future comparable disturbances. - Path back into current capability / S3: after authorized direct installation or Desktop-user approval, the skill is added to the local installed registry so a later Meta-Agent/runtime session can discover and use the revised capability under its current task controls. - Boundary reachability: AgentRuntime registers SessionReviewHook; learning.reviewenabled is an active shipped configuration option, agentwritesrequireapproval distinguishes queue versus direct install, and Studio/Near expose proposal inspection/approve/reject routes. Both paths require configured model/provider access; neither is default-on just because observation is enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model selects substantive future reusable methods, not just a deterministic score; the operator retains the separate approval right in the supervised mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed ultimate-purpose or identity-policy governance path for the local Studio agent-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts about the team&#39;s permanent charter, legitimacy or non-negotiable identity beyond ordinary tasks/permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime identity-level final authority and returned policy choice established in the selected local session hierarchy. - Decision owner: none established for this function; product maintainers, Enterprise administrators and local user are adjacent authorities for different matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator permissions, skill-approval gate, safety policies, user prompts, Enterprise Gateway policies and contributor governance. - Closure path: users permit/deny operations, administrators configure Gateway routing, and skill approvals modify future capabilities; these are operational/safety/adaptation choices rather than a reconstructed identity-level decision loop in local Studio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a Meta-Agent CEO label does not create sovereign identity control, and separate enterprise governance does not transfer to deployed Studio S5. - Evidence: runtime/Enterprise boundaries, Studio tool gates, Meta-Agent identity prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no finding about how the open-source project or enterprise tenants should normatively govern themselves is made.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb/assessments/ag/agenticx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb\">d83ab15 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T17:45:27Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pipecat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat/tree/dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\">dbdf21a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform an environment-facing conversational/agent task by selecting model responses and model-callable tools from live input/context and incorporating tool/service outcomes into subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user utterances, conversation state, tool needs/results, external-service responses, media events and task-specific runtime outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive next response/tool action in the shipped model/tool loop. - Decision owner: the configured LLM actor invoked through Pipecat&#39;s first-party LLM service/pipeline surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context aggregators; tool schemas/callbacks; transports; pipeline frames; jobs; timeouts; service adapters; tracing/metrics. - Closure path: user/environment input enters pipeline → Pipecat constructs model context/tools → model chooses response/tool action → runtime executes/delivers it → observations/results are returned into later context and operation. - Boundary reachability: this is Pipecat&#39;s ordinary supported runtime, not a repository-development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model decision actor leaves transport/execution machinery but removes the open-ended choice of conversational/task action. - Evidence: README.md; llmworker.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider implementation is external, but Pipecat deliberately places the model actor inside its supported operational feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concurrent/interleaved output interference between distinct autonomous LLM workers during a live handoff and ensure that only the selected worker owns the next conversational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, causing both workers&#39; outputs to arrive interleaved; overlapping activation would make ownership of the user-facing turn ambiguous. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide when to transfer conversational ownership from the current autonomous worker to another worker in response to the live conversation. - Decision owner: the currently active LLM worker in the first-party local-handoff mode, through the model-callable transfertoagent tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activateworker; source-pipeline drain; mutually exclusive activation/deactivation; shared worker registry/bus; tool result that suppresses another current LLM run during transfer. - Closure path: current LLM observes conversation → model selects transfertoagent → source worker drains outstanding output → runtime deactivates source and activates target without overlap → subsequent user-facing operation is produced by the selected target worker. - Boundary reachability: the behavior is implemented by first-party worker APIs and exercised by a runnable first-party multi-worker example whose documentation explicitly states that the LLM decides when to transfer. - Distinct S1 units: the active greeter/customer-service LLMWorker and the target support LLMWorker are separate model-driven operational workers sharing the conversation transport through the worker runtime. - Inter-S1 disturbance: without handoff drainage/exclusivity, the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, and both outputs can arrive interleaved. - Attenuating coordination relation: activateworker drains the source pipeline, deactivates it, then activates the selected target so the two workers do not simultaneously own user-facing output. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after the model-selected handoff, the target worker becomes active and receives/acts on subsequent conversation turns while the source is inactive. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism exists to attenuate a documented interference between two autonomous operational workers, and its result changes their subsequent operational participation; credit does not rely on WorkerBus messaging or delegation alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces safe switching, but the semantic choice of when/where to transfer is made by the active model from live conversational evidence. Removing that model decision leaves a callable handoff primitive, not the same coordination judgment. - Evidence: local-handoff-two-agents.py; worker.py; baseworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic WorkerBus messaging/job routing is not used as S2 evidence; credit depends on the concrete interleaving disturbance and the model-selected handoff closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current subordinate agent commitment while preserving a responsive voice/conversation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long-running delegated work can be idle, active, obsolete after a user correction, redirected, cancelled, failed or completed while the conversational front end continues receiving new input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from live subordinate-job state, choose whether to dispatch/update work, inspect current status or preemptively stop the active subordinate commitment. - Decision owner: the voice LLM in the first-party OpenClaw-agent composition; its context exposes sendtoagent, stopagent and agentstatus as model-callable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ActiveJob; bus job requests/updates/responses; job-group cancellation; WorkerRunner; OpenClaw worker; developer messages that return job outcomes to the voice model. - Closure path: voice LLM receives current user input plus returned job state/outcome → model chooses direct response or a current-control tool → Pipecat dispatches/updates, reports or cancels the subordinate job → bus update/response changes ActiveJob and is returned to the voice loop → later current-control decisions use the changed state. - Boundary reachability: the complete path is a runnable first-party multi-worker example built on public first-party worker/job APIs. - Whole-system current view: at the selected voice-plus-agent recursion, the voice regulator tracks the only subordinate operational commitment (jobid, request, start time and later response/update state) while continuing its own user-facing work. - Current-control decision scope: it can create/change the live delegated commitment and preempt it; this is stronger than read-only status or runtime lifecycle cleanup. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM itself selects the exposed dispatch/status/stop tools from current conversational evidence. Runtime code transports/enforces those choices; it does not make an equivalent semantic judgment when the model is removed. - Evidence: openclaw-agent/README.md; openclaw-agent.py; runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the OpenClaw backend decides whether a forwarded update starts a new internal turn or steers an existing one. S3 credit is not assigned to that backend decision; it is assigned to Pipecat&#39;s model-facing authority to observe, issue/update and cancel the current subordinate commitment at this recursion. Generic WorkerRunner end/cancel behavior alone would be constructor lifecycle support, not agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit loop was established that independently inspects current operational claims and returns findings into the live operation/current-control loop for corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pipecat can evaluate bot conversations and report assertion/judge failures, but those evaluation outcomes are separate test/eval results rather than a shipped production correction loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3 at the assessed runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scripted and simulated eval drivers; optional EvalJudge; expectation matching; pass/fail result objects; traces; ordinary worker job responses/errors. - Closure path: no S3-specific corrective closure established. Eval failures can stop or continue an evaluation scenario and are reported to the evaluator/caller, but the reviewed runtime does not feed a distinct auditor finding back into the audited bot&#39;s current work to require revision/retry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate judge exists, but independence alone is insufficient; the missing production feedback closure prevents classification as A or C S3. - Evidence: scriptdriver.py; simulation.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can consume eval failures and implement corrective loops; caller composition is not credited as first-party Pipecat closure without shipped wiring.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established that converts environmental distinctions into persistent changes of Pipecat operating capability/policy used by later work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent conversation memory and dynamic current-session flow/context exist, but no reviewed mechanism closes prospective capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mem0 message storage/search; LLM conversation context; flow transitions and role messages; runtime LLM settings updates; session continuation. - Closure path: no S4-specific closure established. Mem0 stores user/assistant messages and retrieves relevant memories into later context; it does not itself decide or persist a changed skill/tool/policy/capability in response to external/future distinctions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory continuity and current-flow reconfiguration can affect future responses, but the reviewed first-party code does not contain the required sensing → prospective adaptation judgment → persistent capability change → reuse loop. - Evidence: memory.py; flows/manager.py; llmcontext.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream agent can use Pipecat tools to build its own learning/adaptation system; that is outside this standalone runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy loop with legitimate ultimate authority and returned closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bots can have durable system instructions, role messages and application-authored rules, but these are configured operating instructions rather than an S5 adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an S5 witness would require an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy issue to reach a legitimate parent/ultimate authority and the resulting policy decision to return into later operation; no such first-party path was established. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured systeminstruction; Flow rolemessage; tool schemas; application code; provider/session settings. - Closure path: no S5-specific escalation-and-return path established. Pipecat can apply or update instructions supplied by the application, but that is instruction transport/current configuration rather than ownership of identity or ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither static developer-authored identity nor runtime system-instruction mutation by framework/application code establishes a legitimate ultimate-policy decision loop under Profile 0.2.4. - Evidence: flows/manager.py; llmservice.py; uiworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream product owners may govern bot identity out of band; this assessment does not convert arbitrary application configuration into Pipecat S5 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432/assessments/pipecat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432\">2f9af52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft AutoGen AgentChat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/tree/027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\">027ecf0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: stateful agents choose tools/actions and incorporate returned results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: team selection plus Magentic-One&#39;s explicit loop/stall detection and controlled transfer of the next action right suppress uncontrolled repetition/interference among multiple S1 participants. This is more than mere delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: MagenticOneOrchestrator owns a whole-team current facts/plan/progress ledger and has authority to assess progress, choose commitments, reset participants and regenerate the plan when the team stalls. Code-execution approval constrains an operational executor action rather than independently owning this whole-team current-control loop, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the progress ledger is part of the same operational S3 regulator that directs the team. It is not a distinct complementary audit channel with separate reviewing independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: replanning after current-task stalls is inside-now adaptation. No distinct external/prospective intelligence function shaping future organizational posture is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: participant set, instructions, termination rules and ultimate authority remain application/parent-owned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/autogen-agentchat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">data-to-paper</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper/tree/81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\">81df14c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform the environment-facing scientific work needed to transform project data and research state into executable analyses and research artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous datasets, generated-code errors, unexpected outputs/files, statistical-analysis requirements, intermediate product state and manuscript construction requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise analysis/code/content actions in response to current project products, execution results and validation/reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party performer/code-writing agents in the hypothesis-testing workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage runner, CodeRunner, debugger conversers, code extract/check logic, output requirements, product store, data chaining and configured iteration bounds. - Closure path: current scientific products/data -&gt; agent proposes code/content action -&gt; first-party runner executes/materializes it -&gt; output/error/product state returns -&gt; agent revises or advances. - Boundary reachability: these paths are the shipped hypothesis-testing research workflow invoked by the installable application/CLI, not benchmark-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime and guardrails execute/check code, but the context-sensitive analysis/content choice and revision remain model-agent decisions. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py, requestcode.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the workflow contains multiple performer/reviewer roles and research stages, but no concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among distinct S1 units is identified and attenuated by a dedicated coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed stage transitions, product passing, paired conversations, deterministic sequencing and rewind/retry utilities. - Closure path: these mechanisms sequence work and transmit review feedback; they do not close a cross-S1 interference-regulation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interacting agents and state transfer are real, but the reviewed paths are production/review dependencies rather than S2-specific conflict attenuation. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system inside-and-now control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage completion, retries, rewinds, project parameters and review rounds constrain current work, but no first-party autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-project current view plus broad authority over shared resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage flow and retry bounds are primarily encoded in deterministic runner logic and project parameters; local reviewers control product acceptance rather than organization-wide current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DataStepRunner/HypothesisTestingStepsRunner, stage enums, product readiness, retry/rewind helpers, project parameters and UI actions. - Closure path: configured stage/retry conditions alter control flow mechanically; no discretionary whole-system S3 owner is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents make local scientific/product decisions, while the broader workflow sequence is framework-defined. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide a separate challenge channel that reviews scientific/code/writing products and returns concrete corrective feedback before those products are accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible-but-wrong analysis outputs, code/result problems, weak hypotheses/plans, writing defects and other product claims that ordinary performer generation may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently approve a product or issue constructive concerns that force another performer revision cycle. - Decision owner: the separate reviewer LLM conversation in ReviewDialogDualConverserGPT and review-specific subclasses; in code paths, review agents inspect executed outputs/code products separately from the generating/debugging conversation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate ConversationManager, review termination phrase, max review rounds, product extraction/checks, code-review prompts and rewind utilities. - Closure path: performer produces valid candidate -&gt; reviewer receives the candidate/background -&gt; reviewer approves or supplies concerns -&gt; concerns are appended to the performer conversation with explicit correction request -&gt; performer rewrites/revises -&gt; review repeats. - Boundary reachability: the review dialog base class is reused by goal, planning and manuscript-writing review steps in the shipped hypothesis-testing workflow; code review is also embedded in the standard code-product path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is a separate agent conversation and owns the semantic approve/challenge judgment; deterministic loop code only routes/enforces that judgment. - Claim being audited: that a current scientific product/code result is adequate/correct enough to become accepted workflow state. - Ordinary reporting path: performer response plus normal generated/executed product state. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer conversation receives the product and relevant background/output context; code review can inspect created output contents. - Independence boundary: reviewer has its own conversation manager/role and does not share the performer&#39;s assistant role; review feedback is generated independently and then returned. - Who acts on findings: the original performer/code-writing agent revises the product in the next cycle. - Evidence: dualconverser.py, requestcode.py, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: use external literature/novelty evidence to develop or revise future research direction and return the selected direction into the current experimental plan, with both autonomous and Copilot parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing literature may make a candidate goal non-novel, external findings may change relevant hypotheses, and a human scientist may judge the proposed future direction inadequate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in Autopilot, research/novelty agents judge literature-derived novelty and generate/refine the goal/hypothesis; in Copilot, human review can replace/supplement reviewer feedback and require revised research products. - Decision owner: autonomous research/reviewer agents in base mode; human scientist in the supported Copilot parent mode for the reviewed future-direction decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: literature-search agents, NoveltyAssessmentReview, ReGoalReviewGPT, stage return to GOAL, human-review application interactions, product storage and plan handoff. - Closure path: external literature -&gt; novelty/goal review -&gt; autonomous or parent judgment -&gt; if inadequate, workflow returns to goal generation/refinement -&gt; resulting goal/hypothesis/testing plan feeds data analysis and later research stages. - Boundary reachability: literature/novelty/goal refinement is part of the shipped hypothesis-testing runner, and README plus HumanReviewType/review interaction code expose Copilot human feedback through the same first-party workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base mode closes without human direction through model-agent novelty/refinement decisions; the separately supported Copilot mode intentionally transfers the relevant review/judgment right to the human and returns that decision into workflow state. - External distinction: retrieved scientific literature and most-similar-paper evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: whether the current research goal/hypothesis is novel/appropriate enough to warrant future experimentation. - Adaptation option generated: revised research goal/hypothesis and downstream hypothesis-testing plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: failed novelty sets regoal and returns to ScientificStage.GOAL; accepted/revised goal then feeds planning, code analysis and manuscript stages. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project parameters, research goals, human feedback and methodological guardrails constrain work, but no identity-level dispute is routed to an ultimate authority and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: startup/project parameters, user research goal, coding guardrails, reviewer prompts and human-review UI. - Closure path: these controls constrain local research behavior directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; returned policy loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither autonomous agents nor the Copilot human are shown resolving an identity-level policy issue at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/data-to-paper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:11:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CowAgent</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent/tree/7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\">7c55a61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/scheduled/delegated objectives into environment-facing tool/skill work and returned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, model uncertainty, tool results/errors, files/shell/browser/service state, channel messages, and follow-up observations encountered during a trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/skill action, incorporate returned observations, delegate bounded work when useful, or stop with a final response. - Decision owner: the running CowAgent Agent/model actor inside the first-party AgentStreamExecutor loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChatService, AgentBridge, provider adapters, prompt/workspace construction, tool registry, permissions, session persistence, scheduler integration and context controls. - Closure path: each tool result, delegated result or new model observation returns into the first-party executor context and can change the next autonomous action until completion. - Boundary reachability: the credited executor/bridge/tool path is the normal shipped Web/channel runtime and is instantiated directly by standard CowAgent operation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code supplies transport, limits and execution, while the model actor owns the discretionary next-action choice on each loop iteration. - Evidence: pinned agent/chat/service.py and bridge/agentbridge.py; README.md corroborates the shipped planning/tool execution boundary. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider/tool availability, permissions and iteration limits constrain the Agent&#39;s operational discretion but do not replace its next-action judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference between sibling operational Agents when concurrent delegations target the same persistent relay conversation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent handoffs to one source→target relay session would otherwise interleave turns in the target Agent&#39;s transcript and corrupt the shared conversational state used by later delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize admission to that target relay session so exactly one delegated turn occupies it at a time; timeout rather than permit an overlapping handoff when the lock cannot be acquired within policy. - Decision owner: constructor-owned at the assessed boundary. The first-party S2-specific serialization primitive is complete and operational, but the conflict policy itself is deterministic/developer-selected rather than autonomously chosen by an Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-relay relaylock, stable relay-session derivation, delegation roster/ACL, depth/cycle checks, timeout and synchronous result return. - Closure path: a source Agent&#39;s delegation blocks on the relay lock; after the prior target turn finishes and releases the lock, the next delegated target run executes against a non-interleaved transcript and its result returns to the waiting source Agent, changing that source&#39;s subsequent S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: agentdelegate is a shipped team tool attached to normal team turns through AgentBridge; no downstream coordinator or test-only actor is required for the serialization path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents autonomously decide to delegate and select the teammate/task, but once two handoffs conflict the coordination choice is a fixed first-party lock policy. CowAgent therefore exposes a real S2-specific construction path without an autonomous owner of the conflict-regulation decision. - Evidence: pinned agent/tools/agentdelegate/agentdelegate.py and team-session support in bridge/agentbridge.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: other team features such as speaker selection, shared transcripts and ordinary handoff are not separately credited as S2 merely because they connect Agents. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured live CowAgent Agents with separate profiles/workspaces and first-party execution loops participating in one team conversation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping delegated turns to the same target relay session can interleave in the target transcript. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party per-relay lock queues competing handoffs and allows only one target turn at a time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordinated target result is returned synchronously to the delegating source Agent&#39;s tool loop; timeout/failure likewise returns a result that changes the source Agent&#39;s next decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock exists specifically because concurrent sibling handoffs would interfere by interleaving one operational transcript, and it changes admission/timing to suppress that identified disturbance before results feed back to the source Agent.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the currently configured CowAgent installation as a whole by changing which operational Agents are available, which models/capabilities they use, and which live channel instances/teams route work to them. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch between installed operational capacity and the operator&#39;s desired roster, agent availability, model/capability allocation, shared/own knowledge mode, default ownership and live channel/team bindings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update/archive/delete an Agent, make an Agent default, enable/disable it, alter its model/skills/knowledge allocation, or rebind a running channel/team to a different Agent roster. - Decision owner: the authenticated self-hosted operator is the legitimate parent actor in this supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentAdminService, roster revision checks, Web authentication, registry/router rebuilding, scheduler reconciliation, targeted live-runtime eviction and hot channel rebinding. - Closure path: the operator submits a first-party Web/API decision; CowAgent persists it, updates the live registry/router or channel binding, starts/stops the affected schedulers and evicts changed Agent instances so subsequent current work is executed under the returned control decision. - Boundary reachability: the authenticated Web console and its /api/agents administration paths are a standard shipped self-hosted operating surface documented as the unified configuration hub, not a development-only control plane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no standard first-party autonomous Agent is given installation-wide roster/model/capability/channel authority. The decisive current-control choice comes from the authenticated operator; runtime code only validates, persists and enforces it. - Evidence: pinned agent/admin.py, channel/web/api/agents.py, and bridge/agentbridge.py scheduler/runtime reconciliation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this claim is limited to installation-level current capability/resource control. Per-task steering/cancel and permission enforcement alone would not establish S3, and the assessment does not treat every settings edit as an S3 decision. - Whole-system current view: AgentAdminService.snapshot() exposes the installation&#39;s full current Agent roster, default Agent and channel instances; the same authenticated surface operates over that roster rather than one worker-local state. - Current-control decision scope: availability of S1 units, current model/capability allocation, default Agent, channel/team routing and scheduler participation for the running installation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established at the declared installation boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none established as an S3 witness; ordinary tool feedback, run/event recording and Self-Evolution review do not provide a materially independent route to operational reality for checking production claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S3-specific independent audit judgment/feedback right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution/tool events, conversation/run persistence, evolution records/backups, logs and operator inspection surfaces. - Closure path: no independent audit finding→corrective-current-control loop was established; recorded events and evolution changes serve their primary execution/adaptation purposes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no qualifying audit function whose decisive judgment could be assigned to an autonomous Agent. - Evidence: pinned chat/executor event path, conversation storage, Web/admin surfaces, and agent/evolution/ review path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could add an independent verifier or external monitor, but that composed system would be outside this frozen first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert externally observed conversation patterns and capability failures into prospective adaptations of reusable future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user preferences and recurring work patterns, skill defects discovered in real use, reusable workflows not yet encoded as capabilities, and unfinished work whose recurrence or omission would reduce future fit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after an idle conversation, decide autonomously whether there is a clear durable signal and whether to patch an existing skill, create a new reusable skill, consolidate knowledge/memory, or take no adaptation action. - Decision owner: the isolated first-party Self-Evolution review Agent/model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle trigger thresholds, restricted evolution toolset, guarded workspace paths, pre-change backup, snapshot diff/change detection, transactional rollback and evolution records. - Closure path: the review Agent senses conversation-derived external signals, selects and writes an adaptation, the executor commits only real guarded changes, and later normal Agent runs load/use the revised skill/knowledge/persona state; if no clear signal exists it explicitly leaves capability unchanged. - Boundary reachability: Self-Evolution is started by normal AgentBridge initialization, its configuration class defaults enabled, and the standard pinned config-template.json ships selfevolutionenabled: true; the decision actor is therefore reachable in the supported distribution without borrowing a dogfood or contributor agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger timing, backups and write guards are deterministic support, but the model review Agent owns the discretionary judgment of whether a past external interaction predicts future reusable need and which permitted adaptation to make. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/config.py, trigger.py, executor.py, prompts.py, bridge/agentbridge.py, config-template.json and docs/memory/self-evolution.mdx. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple memory consolidation alone is not the credited S4 witness. The positive mapping relies on the separately evidenced prospective skill-adaptation path: detecting a workflow likely to be wanted again or a skill defect exposed in use, autonomously generating an adaptation, and returning that change into later capability. - External distinction: the review consumes user conversation and actual skill/task experience from the environment, including repeated preferences, failures and emergent workflows. - Future / prospective distinction: the evolution prompt explicitly asks whether a workflow is reusable and likely to be wanted again, and documentation defines the goal as creating/fixing skills so they can be reused next time and future conversations improve. - Adaptation option generated: patch a defective skill, encode an emergent workflow as a new reusable skill, consolidate durable knowledge/memory, complete suitable unfinished work, or deliberately make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: committed workspace/skill/knowledge changes are the same first-party artifacts read by subsequent normal Agent operation; operator rollback remains a separate parent intervention path rather than the owner of the autonomous adaptation judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: govern the Agent&#39;s durable identity/personality and highest-level workspace behavioral principles through first-party identity files that are loaded into subsequent system prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: drift between the Agent&#39;s durable identity/behavioral principles and explicit repeated signals about what the assistant should be, plus explicit operator decisions to redefine those identity/policy artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in the autonomous mode, decide whether an explicit repeated identity/personality/style signal is strong enough to justify a bounded AGENT.md identity revision; in the parent mode, authoritatively edit AGENT.md/RULE.md through the authenticated core-file administration surface. - Decision owner: base mode — the isolated Self-Evolution review Agent for the deliberately narrow autonomous identity-evolution decision; parent mode — the authenticated self-hosted operator as ultimate authority over the installation&#39;s core identity/policy files. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution signal criteria and guarded writes/backups; AgentAdminService core-file revision checks and atomic writes; authenticated Web endpoint; prompt/workspace loader; targeted runtime eviction after operator edits. - Closure path: autonomous mode: repeated identity-level external signal → review-Agent identity judgment → guarded AGENT.md edit → subsequent runtime loads the changed persona. Parent mode: authenticated operator reads/edits a core identity/policy file → atomic persistence and cached-Agent eviction → subsequent Agent instance reloads that file into its system prompt. - Boundary reachability: both modes are shipped supported runtime paths: default-enabled Self-Evolution may edit AGENT.md under its standard guard, while the authenticated Web Agents API exposes core-file read/write and immediately evicts the changed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base identity change is not a static prompt or automatic parser; the review Agent must judge whether a rare explicit repeated signal warrants changing its own identity artifact. The separate operator mode retains parent authority to set or override the same identity/policy surface, hence A(P) rather than plain A. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/prompts.py, executor.py, agent/prompt/workspace.py, agent/prompt/builder.py, agent/admin.py and channel/web/api/agents.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission settings, tool approvals, memory/preferences and per-task steering are not credited as S5. The autonomous claim is narrowly limited to the explicit first-party AGENT.md identity-evolution path; operator edits establish the separate parent mode. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: AGENT.md is explicitly defined by the shipped templates as the Agent&#39;s identity/personality and is loaded with RULE.md into the system-prompt context; this is materially different from ordinary task or tool policy. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base (A) — Self-Evolution Agent decides whether the standard rare-signal criterion warrants a durable identity edit; parent (P) — authenticated operator directly owns the authoritative core-file edit and may override/undo Agent evolution. - Return-to-operation path: both modes persist the core file used by prompt construction; operator writes additionally evict the cached runtime, and later Agent initialization reloads the changed identity/policy context.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4/assessments/cowagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4\">a00011a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agno</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/tree/44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\">44219f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: member agents autonomously execute bounded model/tool work and return results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: TeamMode.tasks provides an out-of-box agent-owned mutual-adjustment loop over a shared task board. The leader assigns ownership, prevents duplicate tasks, encodes dependencies, observes member outcomes and can retry/reassign/change the plan; dependency state prevents conflicting/premature execution. This goes beyond one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the same leader has a current whole-team view of tasks, assignees, dependency/blocked state, results and failures, plus authority to create/reprioritize through dependencies, assign/reassign members, execute work, update status and decide overall completion. That is a concrete agent-owned current-control loop over shared operational commitments. Human-in-the-loop paths pause member/tool execution and return the result to the team; they do not independently take ownership of the team-level assignment/plan/completion right, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Agno lets applications compose reviewer/critic members, but the standard team runtime does not supply a distinct sufficiently independent audit channel with alternative operational access and required corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: memory, knowledge, reasoning and learning capabilities can improve team operation but do not by themselves instantiate a distinct external-and-prospective intelligence function coupled against S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: team role, instructions, permissions, approvals and identity are application/parent configured. The tasks leader regulates execution but is not given ultimate agent-owned policy/identity authority or an S3–S4 closure role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/agno.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:08:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Auriga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga/tree/3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\">3e1be75 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue robot-manipulation goals through visual observation, task planning, multi-stage preview/execution, measured action feedback and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object/robot geometry, failed or partial grasps, occlusion, movement slips, constrained arm trajectories and uncertain action completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next physical task action, observation, program, trajectory revision or recovery in light of returned evidence. - Decision owner: primary model-driven Codex actor (named Astra in optional verification documentation), with action authority mediated by the Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: action identity and current-observation checks, native motion planner, gripper guards, single action writer, program review broker, action ledger, wall/budget supervision and the physical simulator. - Closure path: Codex task/plan → robotobserve and robotaction preview → model selects execute → Harness.execute native worker → measured receipt/images/stop reason → model adjusts or terminates episode. - Boundary reachability: normal gateway creates the Codex session and Harness, registers robotaction and connects execution to native RoboDojo worker. The model/tool loop is shipped with documented flags, not merely a benchmark test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static robot safety gates do not select a new manipulation strategy; the actor model makes the task-level decisions even when a hard guard prevents unsafe execution. - Evidence: README execution loop, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct conflict-specific inter-S1 coordination and return is established in the supported robot actor organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dual-arm trajectory interference and concurrent program/native action requests are real actuator/resource hazards but not conflicts among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate independent agent commitments and return a coordination resolution; not evidenced. - Decision owner: one actor plans both arms; deterministic Harness serializes physical actions and rejects stale plans, rather than an S2 coordination owner regulating competing agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage-order dependencies, dual-arm path preview, active-writer exclusivity, plan revision, program broker approval and stop gates. - Closure path: native constraints and the primary actor can alter a manipulation task, but no separate inter-S1 interference-resolution result is fed into distinct operational agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: two robot arms are not independent goal-directed S1 cells; an optional evidence agent audits a claim but does not compete for physical task execution. - Evidence: unified actions, Harness action writer, program review. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-organization current-control authority over a population of operational S1 commitments is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: native action budget, simulator step accounting, active action conflicts, unknown stop confirmation, model transport failures and episode wall limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select systemic allocation/priority/commitment intervention across distinct operations; absent beyond the single robot episode&#39;s work plan. - Decision owner: primary actor owns task strategy; Harness enforces state/timeout/cost constraints; operator defines task/benchmark configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervision.waitturn, action gate, per-action budget, program source grants, GPU resource checks and status/feedback records. - Closure path: failures can stop one episode or prompt its actor to try another action, not a separate whole-current S3 decision that regulates multiple autonomous units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: counters, stop gates and request retry policy are enforcement; removing a hypothetical S3 model leaves them intact. - Evidence: runtime supervision, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: the primary robot actor&#39;s local claim that a selected grasp/lift/transfer/place/release or retreat phase achieved its expected visible result, as distinct from a native worker&#39;s narrower completed execution receipt. - Ordinary reporting path: Harness.execute returns a native action receipt and current measured observation to the primary actor, which can then report success on the strength of its own readings; a tool response alone does not prove object retention or task completion. - Complementary access path: the separately launched Sol reviewer is given the raw linked BEFORE/AFTER permitted RGB images, measured robot state, immutable action IDs/receipts and the actor&#39;s intended outcome as a hypothesis; it checks visual identity/relation independently and returns a structured met/notmet/uncertain verdict with gaps. - Independence boundary: CodexEvidenceAgent creates a fresh ephemeral thread, empty workspace and isolated home, disables memories/tools/hooks, and rejects observed helper tool activity; it is not forked from the acting Codex history and cannot issue motion commands. The actor chooses checkpoint and evidence, limiting scope but not transferring visual judgment to the actor. - Who acts on findings: the primary Astra actor receives visionreview results in its ordinary tool stream and is instructed to resolve unmet/uncertain evidence with fresh observations or changed recovery; the reviewer itself cannot execute or compel a trajectory and the operating loop retains physical action ownership. - Function: provide an independent, evidence-limited second opinion on a manipulation operation&#39;s claimed visible outcome and send discrepancies or uncertainty back to the acting agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic success interpretations of completed motion receipts, wrong target identity, occlusion, unsupported grasp/placement/release claims and partial execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge met, notmet or uncertain for a claimed phase by comparing independent before/after RGB, execution receipts and measured robot state, and specify evidence gaps/recovery feedback. - Decision owner: separate fresh gpt-6.1-sol evidence agent; primary Astra remains owner of the subsequent physical action and the operator decides whether to enable this configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic provenance and schema checks, isolated fresh home/thread, suppressed tools/memory, payload source hashes, shared model budget, exact model assertion and unsupported-positive veto in constrainanswer. - Closure path: actor selects meaningful checkpoint and invokes visionreview(evidencemode=&#39;operation&#39;) → fresh Sol independently examines linked images/receipts and issues bounded verdict and follow-up → tool response returns to the primary actor&#39;s Codex turn → actor instructed to resolve uncertainty or choose a changed recovery before later motions. The auditor never executes robot actions. - Boundary reachability: features.visualreviewmodel=&#39;gpt-6.1-sol&#39; is a documented supported config; the actual Workbench instantiates VisualReviewer(CodexEvidenceAgent(session)), the runtime tool handler dispatches it and returns the structured assessment to the ordinary actor context. Default null mode has no S3 helper; positive vector reflects the first-party enabled operating mode, not default execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh model provides the discretionary visual evidence judgment; deterministic validation limits unsupported assertions but cannot itself interpret ambiguous target retention/phase outcome. Actor authority to choose recovery is distinct from auditor judgment ownership. - Evidence: mode and return contract, Workbench construction, fresh agent isolation, review judgment, actor recovery instructions, tool handler. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: actor chooses evidence/request and need not obey a fixed compulsory verification phase sequence; this is a first-party supported optional independent second-opinion loop, not privileged simulator-ground-truth audit or guaranteed physical correction. No real provider/robot verification was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate externally prospective adaptation process with authorized whole-runtime capability-choice and return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel object geometries, repeated failed grasps, changing camera viewpoints, different robot scenes and future task transfer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: change organizational operating capability based on prospective environmental alternatives; not evidenced at the assessed one-episode recursion. - Decision owner: primary model adapts task actions in the current scene; developers/operators configure perception services, model and robot plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptiveactioncheckpoint failure reminders, observation memory, trajectory cost previews, program synthesis and optional external perception. - Closure path: measured failures can prompt an immediate alternate grasp, viewpoint or trajectory within one task; no distinct environment/future analysis → approved organization-wide capability adaptation → feedback loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive recovery within one operating episode is S1 learning/action selection, not automatically S4 strategic adaptation. - Evidence: checkpoint helper, model-led recovery, unified actions/memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate organizational policy/identity arbitration with legitimate authoritative return is implemented inside the robot episode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robot safety boundaries, model permissions, execution modes, privileged task truth, tool grants and physical stop decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate identity-level commitments and revise binding policy for the whole; not shown beyond fixed configuration and task controls. - Decision owner: developer/operator specifies guardrails and modes; deterministic Harness enforces validation; primary actor decides ordinary manipulation steps within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actor observation redaction, action schema and state locks, program grants, model call budget and sandbox controls. - Closure path: individual actions are admitted/rejected against predefined rules, but no policy-level dispute → qualified authority → ultimate decision → new identity-governing operating rule is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may choose a risky trajectory and receive advisory data, but it does not own the system&#39;s ultimate identity or change invariant control policy. - Evidence: action gate, actor information boundary, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13/assessments/au/auriga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13\">ec6167d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:08:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bread Companion</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion/tree/058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\">058ad15 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous bounded conversation and evidence-task transformation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different user material, missing/conflicting evidence, rejected interpretations and malformed or inappropriate generated candidates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose supported findings, wording, draft structure or a practice question; revise after concrete rejection/validation feedback. - Decision owner: the configured real-agent model within the first-party generation/repair loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompts, selected source segments, schema/evidence/style validators, two-attempt repair, run IDs and client lifecycle checks. - Closure path: validation errors re-enter a new generator attempt; accepted content becomes current findings/card/practice state, influencing later turns. - Boundary reachability: real-agent.generate implements the provider interface used by the deployed server; BreadController submits a current turn and reducer applies only matching epoch/context results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model determines substantive task content and correction, while the controller enforces validity and human save authority. Fixed UI transitions alone could not produce the same discretionary outcomes. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; prompts/agent.ts; domain/evidence.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no general tool-executing autonomy; drafts require user confirmation for persistence. This is not a therapy efficacy or clinical safety claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct operational-unit coordination path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These are one conversational work cell&#39;s pipeline/transport stages and revision-consistency controls. No distinct purposeful operational S1 cells with an inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation relation were found. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/run-controller.ts; services/call-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current organizational control function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These regulate individual requests, versions, service capacity and task consent. A session view or stop button is not by itself whole-system resource/commitment regulation with a function-specific parent decision loop. - Evidence: services/bread-controller.ts; services/run-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts; server/limits.ts; server/admission.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: narrow complementary semantic audit of whether contested analysis reintroduces the user&#39;s denied interpretation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a candidate can pass schema/verbatim/lexical checks yet paraphrase or recommend the rejected interpretation again. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently compare actual candidate with user rejection record and judge semantic consistency. - Decision owner: the model in the fresh rejection-review invocation; runtime parses/enforces its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate review prompt/request, only candidate/rejection/current user data, bounded audit input, conservative verdict parsing and forced retry/fallback. - Closure path: inconsistent/invalid audit adds REJECTEDINTERPRETATIONREPEATED; generator receives explicit corrective feedback; after a second repeated rejection the product returns quote/unknown fallback rather than display the contested interpretation. - Boundary reachability: RealAgentProvider invokes the separate reviewer only for non-listen analysis with rejectedFindings after normal validation; reducer REJECT and context-builder persist/send that rejection record. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent review call supplies semantic judgment beyond the generator&#39;s candidate and deterministic lexical check; parsing is enforcement, not the owner. No independent provider identity is claimed. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; server/providers/rejection-review.ts; domain/reducer.ts; domain/context-builder.ts; domain/evidence.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: same configured model/provider may serve both roles; independence is fresh context and task separation for this narrow consistency claim, not empirical independence from shared model biases. Ordinary chat is unaudited. - Claim being audited: the candidate respects the user&#39;s previously rejected interpretation and its paraphrases. - Ordinary reporting path: generator candidate passes schema/evidence/style and exact-normalized rejection checks. - Complementary access path: fresh semantic comparison of the actual full candidate against persisted user rejection records. - Independence boundary: separate stateless request with dedicated review instructions and no generator hidden reasoning/history; source of correction is user record, not a generator self-report. - Who acts on findings: the provider&#39;s repair loop withholds the candidate, requests revision and eventually substitutes a traceable non-interpretive fallback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective capability-adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These adjust current task content, context and service availability. They do not develop future environment/capability options and return an adaptation decision that changes the harness&#39;s present repertoire. Research/benchmark plans are adjacent. - Evidence: domain/context-builder.ts; domain/preferences.ts; server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity or ultimate-policy decision/return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static identity/permissions and user authority over task/content/save do not supply ultimate-policy arbitration. No identity-level issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate decision owner and returned as a new governing policy. - Evidence: prompts/agent.ts; domain/policy.ts; domain/reducer.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15/assessments/br/bread-companion.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15\">8c0178f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:46:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AskTravel Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness/tree/9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\">9204095 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce researched trip advice and structured itineraries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: destinations, preferences, incomplete data, live tool observations and invalid itinerary arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose searches/playbooks, interpret observations and select/revise the itinerary. - Decision owner: the instantiated travelagent model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangChain execution, first-party tool wrappers, Postgres, retry/compaction middleware and Pydantic validation. - Closure path: tool and validation observations return to the model; presentitinerary persists and displays the chosen plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves storage/search wrappers without discretionary travel synthesis. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/api/openaicompat.py; src/travelagent/tools/itinerary.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: API lifespan constructs buildagent; the public chat router invokes that graph with user context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No supplied regulation of a specific inter-S1 disturbance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tools, independent chats and background upkeep must not be equated with coordinated autonomous work cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread/checkpoint branching, user namespaces, parallel tool execution and idle scheduling. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/threadsync.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-service current resource/commitment decision loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider rate limits and background workload are handled as configured runtime constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry backoff, capped users per maintenance cycle, API state and database persistence. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/retry.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the travel actor&#39;s persisted user-fact claims against raw user statements outside the ordinary chat-production path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misfiled interests/dislikes, contradicted or retracted saved facts and missing user-stated preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether a saved user claim needs add/update/delete/noop and propose the smallest source-supported correction. - Decision owner: the separately prompted structured memory-review model invoked by llmproposer; not applyplan&#39;s deterministic matcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle-chat discovery, checkpoint access, guarded store writes, source-quote checks, sensitive-fact exclusion, MAXOPS and memorylog. - Closure path: the reviewer proposes corrections; accepted operations update the shared fact store, and graph.systemprompt reloads that store on subsequent model calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the reviewer the scheduler and quote checker cannot decide semantic reclassification or contradiction; they only constrain/apply its proposed judgment. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; tests/testconsolidation.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A narrowly scoped source-supported memory-claim audit, not travel-answer verification. Same provider is permitted; trust is limited by selected recent messages, saved-fact evidence, model error, quote matching and excluded sensitive fields. No measured audit effectiveness. - Claim being audited: persisted user facts written by ordinary savememory calls faithfully represent user preferences/statements. - Ordinary reporting path: the travel actor writes summarized facts through savememory and later consumes their prompt projection. - Complementary access path: consolidateuser reads HumanMessage content directly from checkpoints and compares it to the store in a fresh structured review context. - Independence boundary: the producing chat actor neither selects the idle review invocation nor supplies its ordinary narrative as sole evidence; separate reviewer prompt and raw user messages can challenge that actor&#39;s stored claim. Shared provider/store do not establish global independence or coverage. - Who acts on findings: guarded applyplan updates memory and logs before/after; subsequent travel calls reread corrected facts. - Boundary reachability: app.lifespan creates the nonstreaming proposer and runconsolidationloop when default memoryconsolidation is enabled; disabled mode intentionally has no audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: live travel availability and changed user preferences are current-task/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, skill playbooks, memory consolidation and dynamic prompt refresh. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/skills/init.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; docs/PLAN.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate-policy or identity decision-and-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task preferences and data controls are not identity-level organizational disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidelines, sensitive-memory guard, settings and user export/delete endpoints. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28/assessments/as/asktravel-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28\">5ae1eb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:44:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenGenieMaster</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster/tree/8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\">8f6dab2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: primary embodied domain work (e.g. inspect/restock, retrieve, clean, handoff) by agent-guided robot work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task variation, unavailable/unknown objects, visual occlusion, robot capability limits, unexpected failures, interruptions and contradictory task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the in-process Claude coding agent chooses task decomposition, relevant tools, guidance, execution/recovery/handoff and whether to retry, based on live task context and returned observations. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent in the optional first-party ogm agent mode; individual robot policy owns lower-level execution, not the higher-level coding-agent choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, EEP schema, Harness, domain templates, adapters, admission interlocks, task store, completion criteria, typed failure/recovery hints, bounded turn count. - Closure path: goal → model tool decision → Harness.execute/robot → observation, checked outcome and persisted continuity → next model turn or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: documented ogm agent command constructs ClaudeAgent with first-party operator prompt and directly wired EEP tools; optional Anthropic SDK/API availability is an execution prerequisite. It is not necessary to invent an agent in application code. Offline fake-client tests demonstrate tool-loop wiring, not actual paid-model behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing ClaudeAgent leaves ScriptedOrchestrator, which can follow fixed recovery rules but does not retain the agent&#39;s open-ended choice of guidance and recovery under novel user contexts. S1 A belongs narrowly to the shipped optional Claude mode; the deterministic demo does not itself establish A. - Evidence: Claude tool loop, operator instructions, EEP handlers, execution/feedback, CLI agent entrypoint. - Basis: first-party optional agent tool loop with consequential environment feedback. - Confidence: high for code-level reachability, moderate for deployed agent outcomes (no provider E2E run). - Caveats: robot simulator is semantic; physical policy autonomy and real-world safety are not verified; provider model behaviour was not exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen conflicts between distinct embodied S1 work cells over capacity, shared work-item affinity and interrupted handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately assigned robot/policy work cells with their own capability and physical state in a fleet; not two names for the same Claude agent. - Inter-S1 disturbance: assigning one busy robot simultaneously, transferring an unfinished pick–carry–place group while the first robot still holds its object, running dependent steps before prerequisites, or handing off to an incapable robot. - Attenuating coordination relation: Harness.dispatch excludes busy/stopped units, respects group affinity and dependencies; updatetask(assign/handoff) mediates exceptional reassignment. The agent&#39;s operating prompt directs failure-driven handoff and deliberate retry/wait/stop decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assignment and handoff update persistent TaskStore; the next eeptaskget / eepdispatch / execution uses the new robot, group and dependency state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly detects the inter-robot work-item/capacity conflict and blocks/moves conflicting assignments, including held-object restart semantics; a simple shared task list would not do so. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent incompatible robot assignments and unstable cross-robot handoff in shared work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the autonomous Claude agent can choose to wait, hand off/reassign, force-restart affected work items, or route around failures based on robot feedback; deterministic dispatch enforces ordinary constraints but does not itself own discretionary S2 judgment. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent for exception coordination in the supported multi-robot mode; deterministic Harness for bound-preserving enforcement. Routine default dispatch alone would support only constructor/enforcement, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: busy-robot set, capability filtering, group binding, held-object tracking, dependency graph, continuity persistence, readiness checks. - Closure path: two robot work cells create assignment/holding conflict → feedback/continuity visible through EEP → agent chooses handoff/retry/alternate robot → guard updates whole work item → subsequent dispatch/execution observes new ownership. - Boundary reachability: multi-robot scenarios and eeptaskupdate handoff are first-party tools available directly to ClaudeAgent; no custom coordinator is required in that mode. S2 A does not claim the scripted dispatcher has autonomous organizational discretion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the full exception-response choice can be made by the tool-using model; deterministic locks/guards are supporting regulators. Removing the model would retain fixed handoff mechanics but not its context-sensitive coordination discretion. - Evidence: operating prompt, dispatch and busy rules, handoff safeguards, continuity group assignment, handoff regression. - Basis: identifiable multi-S1 interference, conflict-specific guard, model-accessible discretionary recovery and returned assignment state. - Confidence: moderate: source and synthetic test wiring are clear; real multi-robot model behavior is untested. - Caveats: if physical robots are only passive actuators under one central actor, the organizational S2 interpretation narrows; standard simulated policy work cells still retain local execution environments and separate assignments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: provide a current whole-fleet task/priority/resource regulation path, beyond local per-step delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous active tasks competing for finite robot availability, differing priorities, stopped robots and unmet commitments. - Whole-system current view: eeptasklist lists all tasks, eepcapabilities lists fleet skills/status, Harness.busyrobots spans active, paused and interrupted tasks, and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready enumerates all active tasks. - Current-control decision scope: assign resources across all ready tasks, use task priority when selecting the next work item, stop/pause or restart commitments and intervene on assignment. Task priority is chosen at creation, not autonomously renegotiated by a dedicated whole-system governor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a developer/composed operator or future first-party agent policy must decide whole-system priorities, commitments and exceptions; the shipped scheduler executes preselected priorities and capacity rules. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent demonstrably assigned sustained whole-system S3 discretion in the standard operating instructions. The caller chooses priorities and when the scheduler runs; scripts enforce them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: priority-sorted task store, cross-task dispatch, runready highest-priority selection, stop/resume and event log. - Closure path: all-task view + priorities → scheduled fleet assignment/intervention → persistent task/robot state → next scheduling pass. The first-party constructor is real; an autonomous whole-system deciding/feedback actor must still be composed. - Boundary reachability: eeptasklist, priority at eeptaskcreate, cross-task eepdispatch and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready are shipped callable modes. No private benchmark oracle is needed. This is a function-specific S3 path, not mere generic tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scripted runready orders work by caller-supplied priority; Claude&#39;s documented ogm agent goal prompt serves one task and does not establish an autonomous sustained fleet-wide current-control review. Enforcement is not ownership. Human eeptaskupdate calls are local operations, not a distinct operationally closed parent S3 mode. - Evidence: all-task and fleet view, task priorities and dispatch, cross-task busy guard, scheduler runready, tool registry. - Basis: shipped S3-specific whole-current-task capacity/priority constructor with missing autonomous decisive S3 policy. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: no operator-governed (P) whole-system governance closure, budget bargaining, or accountable strategic priorities is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge the robot policy&#39;s own success report and the ordinary step-completion record through a separately callable, more direct evidence check. - Claim being audited: the physical action succeeded and its effect persists (e.g. an item really remains in the intended slot, not just policy-reported success). - Ordinary reporting path: robot adapter execute returns a policy success/outcome and observation; harness evaluates immediate criteria, records execution and updates task status. - Complementary access path: eepverify / recheckstep later calls criterion checks, optionally at close range, through the adapter&#39;s separate check operation; contradictory results mark prior execution records, convert succeeded steps to failed, and reopen completed tasks. - Independence boundary: the verification request and criterion signal are separate from the policy&#39;s initial self-reported success, and can overturn it in the shipped simulator. However physical HttpAdapter.check calls the same configured robot service (/check), with no guaranteed independent detector/operator/credential ownership; the model can also invoke the check itself. This is an explicit complementary constructor, not a demonstrated organizationally independent S3 auditor. - Who acts on findings: the harness reconciles continuity deterministically, and the coding agent can read discrepancies, recover or create new memory; a distinct audit owner/trigger/sampling authority is not supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive policy completion, persistent-effect regressions and false negatives hidden in routine execution summaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor&#39;s selection of when/how to check and corrective judgment is not operationally assigned; the first-party alternative-evidence and contradiction-to-rework feedback path is implemented. - Decision owner: needs first-party-mode integration/developer composition for independent S3 authority; Harness.recheckstep owns deterministic reconciliation, not independent audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: criteria API, alternate camera/close-range flag, contradiction records, task reopening and offline regression tests. - Closure path: policy claim → later direct criterion check → contradiction/reopening and marked evidence → S1 rework and future reflection. Distinct auditor authority is the missing component for A. - Boundary reachability: eepverify is exposed to the built-in Claude and MCP agents and Harness.recheckstep is shipped in the runtime; this is more than a test-only audit, though real detector independence is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a same-loop verifier can provide valuable extra evidence but does not prove a separately controlled independent audit; C recognizes the material constructor without fabricating A. - Evidence: recheck and task reopening, robot adapter direct check, tool eepverify, regression showing overturned completion, criteria. - Basis: separate criterion and corrective-return path, conditional independence at the evidence level, missing independent audit ownership for A. - Confidence: moderate; stronger simulation path than unverified real backend. - Caveats: same backend may correlate failures; normal immediate completion-criterion checks alone would not suffice for S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a capability-adaptation path using observed environmental difficulty and prospective targeted rehearsal, then return learned alternative guidance/coordination into subsequent work. - External distinction: repeated physical-world failures in particular robot/model/skill/object contexts, e.g. occluded targets, look-alike stock, changed item placements and slow handling, compared across execution records and domain catalog. - Future / prospective distinction: capability-gap severity and planned idle-time practice for troublesome contexts before the next customer/work task; this is an opportunity to develop/rehearse future response capacity, not just retry the current failed step. - Adaptation option generated: capabilitygaps groups recent failures, relative attempts and slowness; planpractice generates low-priority repeated pick/return tasks; RuleReflector or optional LLMReflector mines successful recoveries and contradictions into contextual patch instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: practice runs produce execution records → evidence-checked SharedAdmission admits or rejects guidance/recovery/criteria/coordination patches → GuidanceBuilder and chooserobot apply relevant memory on later dispatch/execution; weak non-safety memory can be retired based on outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: foreseeable performance deterioration or new capability demands in observed external object/robot contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: whether and when to run future-oriented practice, what capacity to spend, which gap to prioritize, and whether to make this a standing adaptation program. eepcapabilitygaps(schedulepractice=true) is callable, but no automatic idle/foresight owner and no sustained whole-system adaptation conversation with S3 are assigned in the standard runtime. - Decision owner: caller/developer must compose the autonomous S4 agenda and authority; deterministic gap ranking/practice creation and evidence-gated memory support do not by themselves own prospective choice. An optional Claude agent could call the constructor, but the shipped operator prompt lacks a recurring future-environment scan/idle planning loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution record history, contextual failure aggregation, low-priority practice workflow, rule/LLM reflection, schema/evidence/safety admission, versioned long-term memory and replay tests. - Closure path: environmental difficulty → exposed gap and practice option → caller-scheduled rehearsal → feedback/reflection/admitted patch → changed future guidance/robot preference. The function-specific construction path exists, but autonomous S4 initiative remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: eepcapabilitygaps with schedulepractice and memory tools are actually in the shipped tool registry, not just a README roadmap; rule reflection and admitted patches run in standard simulation and the MCP mode. Physical extrapolation not claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reflective memory loop alone is not S4. The distinct prospective practice option and future capability return make this more than generic memory, while lack of a scheduled autonomous environmental/adaptation governor keeps it at C. - Evidence: capability-gap/practice construction, tool exposure, reflection, admission safety/evidence, memory application, architecture memory path. - Basis: first-party prospective gap/practice-to-later-capability constructor; no complete autonomous S4 decision loop or parent mode. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: gaps are mined from internal execution records of externally situated work, not broad market/strategy sensing. There is no robot-weight self-training; flywheel.py stops at dataset export.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: would decide or revise ultimate identity/mission and balance current robot operations against the future adaptation program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved mission/safety/strategic-policy tensions beyond routine robot-task control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path accepts a genuinely ultimate-policy question, routes it to legitimate authority, records an authoritative identity decision and returns it to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: none evidenced for an active S5 loop; hard-coded safety constraints and user task goals do not constitute an identity governor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: domain safety rules, SharedAdmission rejection of unsafe guidance/memory, robot stop/resume, current-model prompt, human teleoperation example, repo contributor governance. - Closure path: not established at ultimate-policy level; operational stop/resume and evidence-based memory patches affect tasks or safety settings rather than deciding identity or arbitrating S3–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent cannot revise ultimate mission under a dedicated first-party process, and no first-party parent institution/human S5 escalation-return path is supplied. Accepting task goals and protecting immutable safety flags are ordinary operating constraints. - Evidence: operator prompt/safety, admission rules, memory admission cannot weaken safety, robot stop/resume, human backend, research vs repo identity note. - Basis: bounded negative conclusion for reviewed runtime rather than universal impossibility. - Confidence: moderate-high within shipped modes. - Caveats: a real deployment may have human/organizational identity governance outside this repository, but that external authority cannot be inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3/assessments/op/opengeniemaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3\">1c02129 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
